NNAAMMEE
       ppmdraw - draw lines, text, etc on a PPM image


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppppmmddrraaww

       { --ssccrriipptt==_s_c_r_i_p_t | --ssccrriippttffiillee==_f_i_l_e_n_a_m_e } [--vveerrbboossee]

       [_p_p_m_f_i_l_e]

       All  options  can  be abbreviated to their shortest unique prefix.  You
       may use two hyphens instead of one to designate an option.  You may use
       either  white  space  or  an equals sign between an option name and its
       value.



DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppppmmddrraaww draws lines, shapes, text, etc. on a PPM image.  It  is  essen‐
       tially  an easy-to-program front end to lliibbnneettppbbmm's "ppmd" subroutines.
       It lets you create a human-friendly  script  to  describe  the  drawing
       rather than write a C program.

       You  supply drawing instructions with a script, which you supply either
       in a file named by a --ssccrriippttffiillee option or as the value  of  a  --ssccrriipptt
       option.  Here is an example script:

       setpos 50 50;
       text_here 10 30 "hello";
       setcolor black;
       text_here 10 0 "there";
       line_here 5 20;


       This  example starts at Column 50, Row 50 of the input image and writes
       the word "hello" there in 10 pixel high white letters at  a  30  degree
       angle up from horizontal.  Then, from where that leaves off, the script
       writes "there" in 10 pixel high black letters  horizontally.   Finally,
       it  draws a black line to a point 5 pixels over and 20 pixels down from
       the end of "there."

       If you don't specify _p_p_m_f_i_l_e, ppppmmddrraaww reads its input  PPM  image  from
       Standard Input.

       The output image goes to Standard Output.

       ppppmmddrraaww  works  on multi-image streams.  It executes the same script on
       each input image and produces an output stream with one image for  each
       input  image.  But before Netpbm 10.32 (February 2006), ppppmmddrraaww ignored
       every image after the first.

       If you just want to add a single line of text to an image, ppppmmllaabbeell may
       be more what you want.



